\[ 38 + t = 38 + 10 \]

Solving for \( t \):

\[ t = 10 \text{ seconds} \]

Now, with Gerald's improved diet, his speed increased by 10%. This means that for the same distance, he will take 10 seconds less. Therefore, if he originally took 10 seconds to complete the 400 meters, with a 10% increase in speed, he will take \( 10 \times (1 + 0.10) = 10 \times 1.10 = 11 \) seconds to complete the 400 meters.

Alternatively, we can think of it as a percentage increase in speed leading to a percentage decrease in time. Since speed increased by 10%, the time taken for the same distance decreases by 10% as well. So, if the original time was 10 seconds, the new time would be \( 10 \times (1 - 0.10) = 10 \times 0.90 = 9 \) seconds.

Both methods confirm the same result.

Therefore, with Gerald's improved diet, he can run the 400-meter hurdles in \( 9 \) seconds.

